title: Overview
summary: Devtools for ogygia. The dock shows each region on the page, its real JS cost, what crossed the wire and when it woke. The SSR profiler at /__profiler shows why a server render is slow. One Vite plugin turns on either.
related:
  - devtools/dock
  - devtools/profiler/overview
  - testing/overview
---

`@ogygia/devtools` has two tools in one package:

- **The [dock](/docs/devtools/dock)** mounts on each `csr = false` page and shows the island graph: every region outlined in place, the JS it costs over the wire, the values that crossed the boundary, and a timeline of when each island woke. It reads the same events the runtime emits, so what it shows is what happened.
- **The [SSR profiler](/docs/devtools/profiler/overview)** at `/__profiler` answers "why is this server render slow?" by naming the components, functions, network calls and allocations responsible, without timing code of your own.

Wherever the plugin is not listed, all of it tree-shakes away.

## Install

```bash
pnpm add @ogygia/devtools
```

```ts title=vite.config.ts
import { sveltekit } from '@sveltejs/kit/vite';
import { ogygia } from 'ogygia/vite';
import { devtools } from '@ogygia/devtools/vite';
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';

export default defineConfig(({ mode }) => ({
  plugins: [
    ogygia(),
    ...(mode !== 'production' ? [devtools({ profiler: true })] : []),
    sveltekit()
  ]
}));
```

`ogygia.handle()` mounts both tools, so nothing goes in `hooks.server.ts`.

## The plugin options

| Option     | Default | What it does                                                                                                                                                                               |
| ---------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `dock`     | `true`  | The dock. On the dev server a launcher shows on every `csr = false` page. In a build it stays hidden until a browser turns it on with `?og-devtools=on`.                                   |
| `profiler` | off     | `true`, or the profiler's options (`{ secret, path, sampleInterval, … }`, listed under [Options](/docs/devtools/profiler/recording#options)). In production, set `OGYGIA_PROFILER_SECRET`. |

The list is code, so the example above keeps devtools out of production builds; a preview or QA build can keep them and stay invisible to visitors ([In a build](/docs/devtools/dock#enabling-in-a-build-a-preview-deploy)).

## A first look

Start the dev server and open any `csr = false` page. Press **Alt+O** (or click the launcher at the bottom edge) to open the dock, and turn on **Lens** to see every region outlined on the page, with a roster of each one's kind, wake schedule, JS cost and hydration state. Then open **`/__profiler`** and profile the same page by URL to see where its server render spent its time.

To keep profiler reports somewhere other than process memory, give it a store once in `hooks.server.ts`:

```ts title=src/hooks.server.ts
import { env } from '$env/dynamic/private';
import { setProfilerStore } from '@ogygia/devtools/profiler/storage';
import { PostgresStore } from '@ogygia/devtools/profiler/storage/postgres';

setProfilerStore(new PostgresStore(env.DATABASE_URL!));
```

`SqliteStore` and `RedisStore` come from `@ogygia/devtools/profiler/storage/sqlite` and `…/redis` ([Shared report storage](/docs/devtools/profiler/recording#shared-report-storage)). Your own code can name what the profiler cannot see with `span` and `instrument` from `@ogygia/devtools/profiler`, and `mark` from `@ogygia/devtools/profiler/client` in the browser.

## With other packages

**[@ogygia/testing](/docs/testing/overview)** turns what the dock and the profiler measure into test checks. The server under test lists this plugin, and the tests use the fixture:

```ts title=tests/home.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from './fixtures';   // withOgygia(base, baseExpect)

test('home stays light', async ({ page, ogygia }) => {
  await page.goto('/');
  expect(await ogygia.hydration()).toHydrateCleanly();        // the dock's Hydration tab
  expect(await ogygia.profile('/')).toMeetBudget({ score: 80 }); // the profiler's report
});
```

**[@ogygia/cli](/docs/cli/overview)** runs the profiler in CI: `npx ogygia perf --url <origin> --pages /,/docs` renders each page under the profiler and fails when a budget is missed, and `ogygia bisect` finds the commit that made a page slower ([In CI](/docs/devtools/profiler/recording#in-ci-budgets-and-the-first-bad-commit)).

**`ogygia`'s regions** are what the dock is about: each [kind of region](/docs/regions/regions) shows with its schedule, and an island that changed its markup while hydrating is flagged in the Hydration tab.

